From 8b2a6451a888400872902a51fb3839f57beb865f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aubrey Pule Khoza <aubreypk@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 14:35:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] Automate the figures, and check the parity claim on every
 push

Two things that were going to be done by hand once and then quietly rot.

docs/screenshots.mjs drives the real interface through the real API with
Playwright and writes numbered PNGs plus a FIGURES.md of captions. Taking them
by hand gives a chapter whose figures were captured on different days at
different window sizes with different data behind them; this way a figure is
regenerated after a change rather than re-staged. It finds the demonstration
activity by looking for a criterion flagged Thompson rather than hard-coding an
id, so it works whichever curriculum is loaded, and it reads the model answer
from the curriculum file because the API does not expose answers. Fixed
viewport, doubled device scale so figures stay legible at half width, reduced
motion so no transition is caught half-finished, and a narrow-viewport shot as
reflow evidence for Chapter 6.

The workflow reasons over the ontology with rdflib and pySHACL, then puts the
JavaScript that deploys on trial against pySHACL and fails the build on any
disagreement. Every push therefore carries a fresh answer to the question
Section 4.6 raises, rather than one result from one afternoon, and both reports
are kept as artefacts so a claim in the dissertation can be traced to the run
that produced it. It runs on curriculum/example, because the Tshwane material
is not in this repository and CI has no access to it -- which is the split
earning its keep a second time.

Deployment is gated on the credentials being present, so the checks still run
and report cleanly before any Cloudflare account exists. Seeding is opt-in
through workflow_dispatch: the seed drops and recreates every table, and doing
that unasked on each push would wipe whatever the deployed instance held.

Checked: the workflow is valid YAML with the jobs and ordering intended, and
every selector the screenshot script uses resolves against the components --
ids to elements that carry them, classes to rules that exist and markup that
uses them, and accessible names to text actually rendered.

